.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1154 PM PDT Sat Oct 10 2026
(Tonight through Monday)

The northerly ACV-SFO pressure gradient has increased to 5.9 mb
while the onshore SFO-SAC pressure gradient is 1.0 mb. Surface
winds are largely gusty from the northwest over the coastal waters
in response to the northerly pressure gradient, however wind
favored gaps and passes mainly in the East Bay are indicating
ongoing gusty winds as well. 24 hour temperature trends are mostly
cooler across the forecast area as cold air advection is combining
with nocturnal radiative cooling. Daytime highs Sunday and Monday
will be in the 60s/70s, warmest inland. Lows will be in the 40s/30s,
coldest far inland valleys, except near 50 along the immediate
coastline and bays.

Spotty very light measurable rain and/or drizzle (less than 0.10")
is possible across southernmost Monterey County due to a surface
to lower/mid level low pressure circulation (it will be weakening
since surface pressures are forecast to increase) moving southward
along the Big Sur coast Sunday night and Monday. Dry weather will
prevail over most of the forecast area through early week.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1154 PM PDT Sat Oct 10 2026
(Monday night through next Saturday)

The forecast area will remain under cool and dry conditions
Tuesday with a daytime warming trend developing mid to late week.
Thursday to Saturday daytime highs are forecast to return above
mid October normal with highs in the 80s to lower 90s. 500 mb
ridging will advance eastward over our forecast area mid to late
week, bringing a return to warmer daytime temperatures.

